  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday Night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1135 AM CDT Sat May 9 2026\n\nAn upper trough accompanied by a cold front will be moving through\nthe Mid State Sunday night with a medium chance for mostly post-\nfrontal showers and perhaps a rumble of thunder. Atmospheric\nmoisture and other parameters look unimpressive, so we are not\nexpecting much rain. Most areas are showing less than one quarter\ninch. A few storms could be in the mix, but we do not expect any\nsevere storms. \n\nThe cold front will exit the area Monday morning, allowing for drier \nand cooler air to move in behind it. Highs on Monday will be in the \nmid-60s along the Plateau and into the low-70s elsewhere. Another \nsystem moves through the Ohio Valley late Tuesday into Wednesday,\nbut it brings us just low chances for light rain followed by \nanother slight dip in temperatures. Looking ahead to late week, \nthe eastern U.S. trough will begin to lift out as ridging from out\nwest expands eastward. This will set us up for warmer than normal\ntemperatures by next weekend with highs reaching the low to mid \n80s. Some passing waves could bring low-end rain chances, but no \nsignificant rainfall or severe wx is expected.",
